'''Joan Ernesto Lacunya de Sants''' (12 March 1584 – 22 May 1642), also known by his common name, '''Ernesto Lacunya''', was a [[Volisania]]n explorer, navigator, and merchant who established the trading hub of [[Cinc Parets]] on the island of [[Dagit]]. Born in the decades immediately following the [[Great Disappointment]], Lacunya's career was defined by his efforts to secure Volisanian trading routes in the [[Shendan Ocean]] against encroaching [[Fosia]]n commercial fleets and their highly organized Sanese mercantile networks.

He is best remembered in [[Sanese people|Sanese]] and Volisanian historiography for his fateful 1615 shipwreck on the coast of Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]). Rather than sparking a conflict, his honorable conduct toward the second shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], resulted in a landmark diplomatic treaty that recognized the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a sovereign equal to the Volisanian crown, fundamentally altering the geopolitical landscape of Adonia and establishing the Cinc Parets–Okami trade corridor.

==Early life and motivations==
Lacunya was born on 12 March 1584 in the United Kingdoms of Volisania. He grew up in a maritime culture that had been profoundly shaken by the Great Disappointment of 1564—the shocking revelation by the [[Expedició del Ponent]] that the New World was already being heavily colonized from the west by Fosian powers like the Kingdom of [[Zong]].

As a young naval cadet, Lacunya was heavily influenced by the prevailing Volisanian anxiety regarding the Shendan Ocean. The Fosian colonies in the New World, particularly Tamsui, were frequently garrisoned by terrifyingly effective mercenaries from the war-torn Sanese archipelago. These warriors, armed with matchlocks funded by New World silver, were widely referred to in Volisanian literature as the Shendan Demons. Driven by a desire to establish alternative trading routes and secure safe maritime passage for his homeland, Lacunya dedicated himself to cartography and deep-ocean navigation, intent on pushing Volisanian commerce directly into the contested waters of the far west.

==Establishment of Cinc Parets==
In 1605, Lacunya received a royal trading license to lead an expedition across the Shendan Ocean to establish a strategic trading post. Bypassing the heavily fortified Fosian settlements on the western coast of Lurandia, Lacunya's fleet navigated further west, eventually discovering a vast, resource-rich archipelago.

Unlike the aggressive colonization efforts favored by Fosian powers, Lacunya pursued a different approach. Recognizing Dagit's immense strategic value as a neutral trading midpoint, he established peaceful relations with local leaders and constructed Cinc Parets (Five Walls)—a massive, fortified trading warehouse complex that would serve as the cornerstone of Volisanian commerce in the region. The five walls were designed not as military fortifications, but as secure enclosures for valuable goods awaiting trade.

Rather than occupying the islands, Lacunya invited Dagitin merchants, Volisanian traders, and eventually representatives from the Kingdom of Fenia, the Kingdom of Zong, Mitoyagi (Okaiken), Nanami, and Yanbaru to conduct business within the walls of Cinc Parets. Under his fair administration, the settlement quickly grew into a bustling, cosmopolitan hub for trans-oceanic trade. Dagit remained an independent territory with its own governance; Lacunya's presence was purely commercial.

Centuries later, following the growth of Dagitin nationalism, Cinc Parets became the symbolic heart of Dagit independence. The original five walls still stand today and have become a national monument representing Dagit's proud history of neutrality and commerce.

==The Yanbaru expeditions==
Seeking to expand trading networks and map the maritime borders of the Shendan trade routes, Lacunya launched several expeditions northward between 1610 and 1613, arriving at the island kingdom of Yanbaru.

Yanbaru was a critical maritime chokepoint, culturally distinct but heavily pressured by both the Kingdom of Zong and the southern warlords of Mitoyagi. When Lacunya arrived, he found the Yanbaru ports hosting displaced Sanese pirates and rogue merchants utilizing the islands as a staging ground for raids on Fosian and Volisanian shipping alike.

Rather than engaging in costly naval confrontation, Lacunya utilized his diplomatic skills and the promise of lucrative trade agreements to convince the mercenary bands to cease their raids. He established the Treaty of Naha, a localized agreement with the Yanbaru authorities that permitted Volisanian merchant vessels to utilize the ports for resupply in exchange for military protection against the Zongese fleets and a percentage of trade revenues. This successful diplomatic maneuvering in Yanbaru gave Lacunya his first profound insight into the complex, honor-bound martial culture of the Sanese people—an experience that would save his life just a few years later.

==The Mitoyagi shipwreck of 1615==
{{main|Okaiken-Dagit Relations|Honda Shigetomo}}
By 1615, Lacunya's trading enterprise had grown substantially, and he prepared to return to Volisania to report on his successes in Dagit and Yanbaru. He departed aboard the heavily laden merchant vessel ''Matricia''. However, while navigating the turbulent waters north of Yanbaru, the fleet was caught in a massive late-season typhoon.

Blown hundreds of miles off course, the Matricia ran aground on 31 October 1615 on the treacherous coast of Tosaki, a village in the Musashino province of Mitoyagi. Lacunya and his 175 surviving crew members found themselves stranded deep within the territory of the newly unified Honda Shogunate.

Having heard stories of the Shendan Demons for decades, the Volisanians expected to be slaughtered. Within hours, the beachhead was completely encircled by 1,200 elite cavalry and arquebusiers led personally by Shogun Honda Shigetomo, known across the land as the Punisher. Recognizing that resistance was futile and recalling his experiences in Yanbaru, Lacunya waded into the surf and offered his own life to the Shogun in exchange for the safety of his crew.

This display of martial honor profoundly moved Shigetomo. The Shogun ordered his forces to stand down and took Lacunya and his men back to the capital of Okami as diplomatic guests. During their month-long stay, Lacunya and Shigetomo engaged in extensive dialogue regarding the Great Disappointment, the nature of trade and empire, and the balance of global power. Lacunya realized that Mitoyagi was no longer a fractured land of rogue mercenaries, but a highly centralized, sovereign empire with sophisticated trade networks.

Before departing for Dagit on a repaired vessel, Lacunya successfully negotiated a foundational treaty. He convinced the Volisanian crown to recognize the Honda Shogunate as a diplomatic equal, permanently categorizing Mitoyagi as an off-limits sovereign state rather than a target for commercial exploitation. This agreement secured Okaiken's borders and established a lucrative, state-controlled trade network between Okami and Cinc Parets.

==Later life and legacy==
Lacunya chronicled his extraordinary experiences in his highly influential 1628 memoir, ''Diario de un náufrago: Mis viajes a Dagit y las islas Santos'' (Diary of a Castaway: My Travels to Dagit and the Sanese Islands). The book became a sensation in Volisania, completely reshaping [[Illypnia]]n perceptions of the Far East from a land of savage demons to a realm of complex, honorable empires.

He returned to Cinc Parets in his later years, continuing his work as a merchant and serving as an informal advisor to young Volisanian traders venturing into Shendan waters. He died of natural causes in Cinc Parets on 22 May 1642. Today, Lacunya is celebrated in both Dagit and Okaiken as a masterful diplomat who bridged the gap between the Illypnian and Sanese worlds, preventing conflict through mutual respect and establishing the foundation for centuries of profitable trade.

